Lisandro Dami=E1n Nicanor P=E9rez Meyer wrote:
> Any feedback will be kindly appreciated.
>  =20
I've always thought there is something fundamentally wrong.

What is qreal supposed to be used for? If it's supposed to be used for=20
things where float would be adequate then shouldn't it be float on all=20
platforms? If it's supposed to be used for things where float is=20
inadequate then why isn't it double on all platforms?

The current situation leads to people who develop on i386 and amd64=20
(which is most developers) assuming that qreal is equivilent to double.=20
Then we try and build it on arm* and float gets used. Sometimes this is=20
ok because there was really no reason for the developer to be using=20
double precision in the first place and there were no incorrect=20
conversion assumptions. Sometimes this causes build failures which can=20
be difficult to resolve* and I bet in some cases it causes more subtule=20
bugs. It also goes against the principle of offering to the greatest=20
extent possible the same functionality on all platforms.

Making qreal double on armhf but leaving it as float on armel will mean=20
that canonical are no longer forced for fix bugs surrounding it. I'm=20
sure canonical would like that but I also suspect it may cause severe=20
degredation in the maintainance of QT software on those ports that stick=20
with qreal.

On the other hand presumablly there would be a performance hit from=20
switching from float to double, especially on platforms which use=20
software floating point (I presume that was why whoever controlled qt at=20
the time made qreal float on arm in the first place).

I found some older upstream discussion on what to do about float at=20
https://groups.google.com/forum/#!topic/qt-project-list-development/dPcP3=
NASY1k=20

I also found some discussion at=20
http://comments.gmane.org/gmane.comp.lib.qt.qt5-feedback/700 saying that=20
on coretex A8 double was significantly slower than float.

Finally has there been any discussion with other linux distros. This is=20
obviously something that impacts the ABI in a big way so ideally it's=20
not something where we want different distros doing different things.

* See scribus for an especially nasty example.


--=20
